<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#roman-citizenship">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman citizenship</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as civitas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roman citizenship, civitas, was the privileged political and legal status of free individuals in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, governing their rights in law, property and government. There were several kinds, shaped by gender, class and political ties, and citizens owed duties to the state, such as military service, and were recorded in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> every five years under the Republic. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelve-tables">Twelve Tables</a> of about 449 BCE are the oldest surviving document of its rights. Citizenship was extended to the Italian allies around the Social War, and in 212 CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutio-antoniniana">Edict of Caracalla</a> gave it to all free men of the empire, with some exceptions.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Citizenship could be acquired in several ways. A person was born a citizen when both parents were free Roman citizens; others earned it through public service, such as serving in the non-Roman auxiliary forces. The principle that a person could become a citizen by law rather than birth was embedded in Roman mythology: after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romulus">Romulus</a> defeated the Sabines, he promised the war captives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> that they could become citizens.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Besides the full citizens, the cives <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a>, there were other groups. The Latini held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> rights, including the right to migrate but not the right of lawful marriage with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, and socii were citizens of allied states who received certain legal rights in exchange for military service. Provinciales and peregrini had only the rights of the ius gentium, the rules common to nations under Roman rule.</p><p>Citizens could sue and be sued, appeal court decisions, and be tried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> if accused of treason. After the Porcian Laws of the early 2nd century BCE a citizen could not be tortured or whipped, and no citizen could be sentenced to crucifixion. Women had a limited form of citizenship: they could not vote or stand for office, but could own property, do business and obtain a divorce, and normally remained under the guardianship of a father or male relative. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedmen">Freedmen</a> were not automatically citizens, but their children were born free citizens; the father of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a> was a freedman.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Growing dissatisfaction among the socii with their rights and with the manpower demands of the legions led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a> of 91 to 87 BCE, in which the Italian allies revolted. The Lex Julia of 90 BCE granted the rights of the cives <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a> to Latini and socii that had not taken part in the war or would cease fighting at once, and the grant was extended to the Italian allies when the war ended, except for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisalpine-gaul">Gallia Cisalpina</a>.</p><p>Citizenship also served as a tool of foreign policy. Colonies and allies received graduated grades of it, which kept their attention on Roman culture and gave the conquered a stake in the system. It also allowed Roman rulers to cooperate with local elites in the provinces. The Book of Acts indicates that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-apostle">Paul the Apostle</a> was a citizen by birth, which had considerable bearing on his career, and the Digest of Emperor Justinian preserved the court rulings and legal writings that are the basis of knowledge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roman citizenship” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
